.TH CURSOR 1
.SH NAME
cursor \- make the console's cursor a block or a line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cursor
block | line
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cursor block
makes the console's cursor a block the height of a character;
.B cursor line
makes it the usual line under the character again.
.PP
The cursor's shape belongs to the display adapter, not to the screen,
so it is the same on all of the console's screens. cursor sets it in
the adapter's 6845, which keeps the first and last scan lines of the
cursor in its registers 10 and 11, as the IBM PC's BIOS sets them: a
line covers a character's last two scan lines (6 and 7 of the 8 on a
color adapter, 11 and 12 of the 14 on a monochrome one), and a block
runs from the top of the character down to the same last line.
.PP
To have a block cursor every time you log in, put
.B cursor block
in your .login or .profile.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"only on the console" when neither the standard input nor the standard
output is the console. The exit status is 0 when the cursor was set, 1
when it could not be, and 2 after a mistake in the argument.
.SH SEE ALSO
setcolor(1)
.SH HISTORY
The IBM PC's BIOS sets the cursor's shape through the same registers,
with function 1 of its video service, interrupt 10h.
.B cursor
is this system's.
.SH AUTHORS
cursor was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
